chard pearl wrote this clean break  memorandum which basically argued  for israel to go into iraq uh that was  96 and then they came up with  this uh uh  argument to clinton to uh basically  declare war on iraq i think it was 98 or  whatever and  that became part of u.s policy although  uh uh not the war part just the uh the  policy of regime change at some time so  i mean the the push has been there right  all through the 90s right and and i  think what they were doing prior to  going into the war was essentially  rationale shopping you know one day  would be wmd the next day it would be  democracy the next day it would be  fighting terrorism you know and it's  like what works and at the end of the  day well uh wolfowitz himself said at  one point that they settled on wmd  because it seemed like that was the  uh the best way to argue for the war  right and the europeans said it i talked  to some european leaders at the time and  they said look the only reason we're  going to support this is wmd and so it  was like okay well if that's going to  work we'll make it work but apart from  that decision i think the seminal  mistake of the bush administration so  far has been not sending enough troops  into iraq the decision and it's not like  the information wasn't there it's not  like they didn't have the intelligence  to know they needed more it's not like  precedent didn't say they needed more  it's not like the army chief of staff  general shinseki wasn't saying we needed  more  or the state department wasn't laying  out a clear plan for what was needed on  reconstruction  rumsfeld  arrogantly  essentially said no we don't because  that was part of his original uh plan on  coming into the pentagon to reorganize  it to downsize it to focus more on the  technology and so forth right and people  didn't don't realize now or don't  remember very well right now that he was  almost on the way out when september  11th came up right he was the most  likely guy to leave town early because  transformation wasn't going well and he  had alienated the military brass to such  a huge extent but you know you know  while the technological transformation  that he talked about has a lot of appeal  and a lot of value in certain kind of  war  when you're stabilizing a country it's  boots on the ground  and there is nothing else that can make  the kind of difference as as as as  soldiers  moving around door to door and if you  don't have enough soldiers you can't  contain the problem now  i think there are a lot of reasonable  cases to be made that uh you know iraq  is so fragmented uh that  it it might be impossible to contain the  problem anyway  but certainly going in under strength  is represents a deliberate decision to  unlearn many of the lessons of the  recent past and  and that decision lies heavily on the  doorstep of rumsfeld with the  collaboration of cheney  and  the background a lot of these people as  you mentioned rumsfeld's not really a  neocon he's a pragmatist who  has been pushing for years for the u.s  to expand militarily around the world  but he's not really an ideologue  but rumsfold and cheney and and all the  rest they um  and a lot of the neocons that were both  inside and outside the administration  were arguing at the very beginning that  this was going to be a very easy war  cakewalk used to be talked about how the  iraqis would come out with  flags and  um  it'd be sort of like  you know the the allies in paris in 1945  and so forth so  um that completely was uh uh wrong and  um  part of the reason i think uh and i  don't know what your view is but these  are people that spent most of their  careers in in office jobs or in  think tanks and so forth none of them  served in the military a number of them  disdained the military when like cheney  when he was  during the vietnam period  and all of a sudden they're faced with a  real war and it's not going the way that  they have planned it well they also you  know they didn't listen  to the people with the military  experience the united states military  has at his leadership level some of the  most remarkable people in washington  some of the most experienced and  intelligent people in washington and  they were deliberately being turned off  but i think you know it goes beyond that  i think they overreacted to 9 11 in some  fundamental ways this led to decisions  to violate not just military doctrine  but principles that are core principles  that have led to american strength in  the world and you know when  guantanamo was set up i have a friend  who is down there who got a memo from  the department of defense signed by the  secretary of defense saying you have  more latitude than you  uh traditionally would have had while  you are  interrogating these prisoners  and abu ghraib is another example of  that and i think you know compromising  america's moral authority  just like compromising our economic  strength  reveals a deep misreading of the nature  of national security policy national  security policy is not about identifying  threats and responding to them national  security policy needs to be about  identifying sources of our strength and  cultivating them and then using those  sources of our strength in order to  uh help us protect against threats and  that's how we won the cold war not  containment but you know as you know  george kennan writing in the long  telegram in you know in 1947  said  if we are to defeat the soviets it will  be by cultivating a strong economy and a  strong society because that will enable  us to do what we want to do and it will  draw people towards us and these guys in  their desire to make a mark to react  essentially  undercut the two pillars of our strength  our our economic strength and our moral  authority or our political strength  uh exactly and i agree 100 that  i think the key thing is to show an  example to the world that we should be  something to be admired to be looked up  to and that will cause populations  around the world to want to be a  democracy but right now when uh when you  have a lot of the world seeing uh you  know the the ultimate and dirty laundry  being hung up uh avograve and guantanamo  and so forth it's having the opposite  effect uh just this week there was a  report in the washington post or the the  new york times rather  i said iraq may be prime place for  training of militants uh cia report  concludes  um so what we're creating is not uh  a safer world we're creating a more  dangerous world we're creating a  haven for terrorists to learn their  trade  to find better ways to blow up cars to  blow up buildings and  i think that was the reaction of the  public  when they did a poll that the  public came out saying that they feel  the  um the war in iraq is making the world a  more dangerous place now does this have  any effect on on the people uh in the uh  national security council and the the  inner workings of the bush  administration and bush himself or or  does this  sort of  roll off their backs like rain well i  don't think it's rolled off their backs  like rain i you know i i i think that  you can see in the first six months of  the second term  with the sort of charm offensive  globally  where you know condoleezza rice and  laura bush have made more trips  internationally  by far than donald rumsfeld or dick  cheney  that we're trying to offer a new face to  the world uh i think that you know the  the effort to emphasize africa in the  upcoming g8 talks is a desire to show  look we have a conscience and we can  reach out  to the rest of the world  but i think a lot of chickens are going  to come home to roost in the next few  months whether it's the iraqi referendum  uh or the progress of insurgency against  the united states or the elections uh in  the palestinian territories and whether  hamas actually wins those elections  and also i might add  how the u.s economy fares when we seem  to not care about building up deficits  and spending money on war  despite the fact that borrowing that  money from the few countries in asia  that are willing to lend us that money  puts us in a very very precarious  position uh right down to main street  you know interest rates go up  people can't pay their mortgages  housing bubbles pop  the economy suffers and our national  security suffers you know all of these  things are connected and i think we have  made as many mistakes of commission  as we have of omission well one of the  problems i think uh all along has been  the the lack of attention that the  public was paying to this uh this war  and a lot of the things that we were  doing it seemed to be um and the press  to large degree was putting it on the  back back pages um only now they're  starting to um  really get uh people's attention the  polls are starting to drop  i've always thought that uh one of the  the problems is that uh  the public hasn't has very little  invested in this war  it's all uh people who are volunteers  there's no draft  and they're not paying anything and  their taxes for it their taxes are  supposedly being lowered  and one way to avoid these  wars in the future i think wars that may  eventually generate into  attacks in syria or iran or wherever is  to uh  have a draft and  no deficit spending on um on a war you  pay for that war that year as a  surcharge of your income tax then i  think people would start paying a little  bit more attention to  whether we're going to a war for a good  reason or a bad reason well i think  that's true i think that people don't  feel the pain except for the ones who  are closest to the war  i think we've moved away from the  constitutional ideal of the congress  approving movements to war because we  characterize things somewhat differently  we leave the decision with the executive  branch and i also think in the wake of 9  11 what happened in the united states  was that if you were to oppose this  action you were equated with becoming  traitorous that you were not patriotic  you were not supporting the united  states debate was quashed at just the  moment we needed the most debate now i  personally think there are plenty of  reasons to go into iraq uh i i i think  the time was not right  i think the rationale was not right i  think the method was not right but i  think iraq was a bad place and certainly  saddam was a bad guy i think there are  plenty of reasons for us to intervene  even unilaterally when we see a dramatic  threat to the united states an imminent  threat and that's been u.s policy for a  long time what i see as the principal  problem however is stepping away from  the ideals that were framed in the  earliest days of the republic and  reinforced after the second world war  which is that whatever power is accrued  by individuals or states needs to be  submitted to a system of law because  that's essentially what we're about  george washington could have been king  he chose not to be he said that's not  why we fought i will submit myself to  the system of law i will leave office  voluntarily i no man is above the system  at the end of the second world war we  said although we are the last great  surviving power we are  untouched by the war 50 of world trade  passes through our border  we will create a global system that we  will participate in because we realize  what happens when nation states act  alone in their narrow national interests  and these principles were completely  abrogated in the wake of 9 11  because we assessed the threat as being  so great  that  working within systems building  communities building up systems of law  was no longer in our interests and i  think that that played into the hands of  the terrorists i think it weakened us  and and i think that that is the  essential change that needs to take  place over the course of the years to  come is that we need to again convince  the world  and believe in our own hearts that it's  on our interest to have a system of law  where our interests are protected but so  are everyone else's  well it seemed to be uh  an issue back uh when we were going into  war was uh the question of whether wmd  actually existed in in iraq and  uh people uh you know if you look back  at the uh that period of time we had  more intelligence people on the ground  in iraq there than anyplace else in the  world at that point they were going all  over looking  for wmd  and uh what i think people seem to  to  fail to  think about was the  the push to get those inspectors out  there to put the the troops in there  which is the complete reverse of what  you want to do if you're an intelligence  officer you want to keep the  intelligence people there on the ground  as long as possible collecting  intelligence and finding out what's  going on  and not pull them out and and put the  troops in uh and  that was whether you wanted the war or  not that was a huge blunder i think well  i think that's right and i think you  know the 911 commission said  uh that the nsc  uh was virtually dysfunctional in the  run up to 911 with regard to how it was  using intelligence and how it was coming  up the system i think that the changes  we've made in the intelligent system  since 911 are also very revealing you  know the national security act of 1947  created the director of central  intelligence so you had a central figure  to coordinate all the intelligence  agencies it didn't work because the  president didn't empower that figure to  do what he was supposed to do so what  was the solution  create another one put in another layer  there's no business in the world that  would respond to a failure of management  by creating a new layer of management  because that only makes it harder to  coordinate and furthermore the place  where we really needed intelligence  reform was on capitol hill where you  need to change the process of  authorization appropriation of funds  which is what creates the stovepipes and  those stove pipes create the  institutional separations that make  communications very difficult and we  didn't do that so again we've sort of  paid lip service to the notion of  creating a more effective intelligence  apparatus but we haven't done the steps  that are necessary to actually address  the problems that exist within it so i  wouldn't expect that you're going to see  it work better in the future until we're  really willing to tackle some of these  harder issues so you see the the new  director of national intelligence more  or less just as a sort of redundant  additional layer in the intelligence  community well i think that's what it's  amounting to now i mean the director of  national intelligence does the daily  briefing for the president instead of  the cia director the director of  national intelligence handles the budget  process instead of the cia director the  director of national intelligence is  building a bureaucracy of a thousand  people around him  which is not exactly going to speed  communications nor without these changes  that we've talked about is it going to  deal with the cultural issues of  integration of jointness we need on the  civilian side of the government the kind  of jointness  in interagency operations where people  in the fbi have to work in the cia and  people in the cia have to work in the  treasury or the state department and so  forth like you have on the military side  in order to get the system to work but  when that happened in the military side  through goldwater nickels in the late  80s it was because  you couldn't be promoted they they made  it so that it was critical to your  career path in order to promote that  jointness until we get that kind of  incentive system in place  you're not going to solve this problem  within the intel side or between the  intel side and the crime fighting side  or among all the civilian agencies  uh in the couple of minutes we have left  uh  what's your view on the uh the downing  street memos a  number of people in the press put it at  first and uh they were very slow in in  uh  and actually uh commenting or doing  stories on it and um  a number of other people think that  they're in essence  a smoking gun or equivalent to something  like the pentagon papers or whatever and  again these memos one of the key things  the memos show is that there was a  um  an effort according to the  head of british intelligence who was  attending a meeting in washington um  to  wrap the uh intelligence around the  policy rather than the other other way  around  which is not the way to do business  it's not the way to do business you know  i i you know i have the reaction of the  downing street memo of uh  uh you know  claude raines and casablanca you know  i'm shocked shocked you know i mean  everybody knows this  everybody knows that the administration  had this agenda everybody knows they  were rational shopping everybody knows  they shaped  some of the intelligence to support  their  conclusions  i think we need to be intellectually  honest though  this is not the first administration to  do this you're one of the great students  of intelligence out there and  administrations do it regularly i saw it  happen in the clinton administration  intelligence would come in saying you  know aristide was a bad guy and people  would immediately you know put their  hands over their ears and they say oh we  don't want to hear that because that's  not consistent with our policy the  difference here though is we have people  dying every day over in iraq based on  that i i agree that there is an enormous  issue of degree but  and and i'm not excusing it i'm merely  saying this happens all the time  you know at the end of the day you elect  people because you think they're going  to use the intelligence properly and  we have not created a big penalty for  these people in using the intelligence  improperly and until it sort of  resonates to the electorate and they say  no sorry we're not 